  "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n3{e}^{x+1}+1=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{x+1}+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{e}^{x+1}+\\cancel{1}-\\cancel{1} & =0-1 \\\\ 3{e}^{x+1} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides of the last equation by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{x+1} & =-1 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any solutions since its left-hand side is always nonnegative, while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no roots.",
